Make peios a target GCC knows by name rather than one it merely falls through
to, and give it an identity macro.

Peios is the Linux kernel ABI plus a glibc userland, so `x86_64-pc-linux-peios`
matching config.gcc's `x86_64-*-linux*` glob and picking up the generic
GNU/Linux configuration is not an accident to be corrected -- it is the right
answer, and this patch keeps it.  All it adds is one header on the end of
tm_file, which adds `__peios__` on top of the predefines already made.

Both questions need answering.  "Is this Linux?" stays yes -- __linux__,
__unix__ and __gnu_linux__ are untouched -- or every piece of Linux software
would need teaching about Peios.  "Is this Peios?" becomes answerable, which
it currently is not, and has to be: the registry and KACS are reachable only
through dedicated system calls, so no amount of feature probing finds them.

This is the same shape Android uses (linux-android.h adding __ANDROID__), and
our triplet already copies Android's: kernel stays `linux`, the final field is
the userland.

--- a/gcc/config.gcc	2026-08-11 23:25:57.732861160 +0100
+++ b/gcc/config.gcc	2026-08-11 23:26:22.120812076 +0100
@@ -3770,6 +3770,15 @@
 	;;
 esac
 
+
+# Peios: Linux with glibc, plus its own OS identity macro.  The generic
+# GNU/Linux configuration selected above is correct for Peios and is kept
+# as is; this only appends the header that adds __peios__.
+case ${target} in
+*-linux-peios*)
+	tm_file="${tm_file} linux-peios.h"
+	;;
+esac
 if [ "$target_has_targetcm" = "no" ]; then
   c_target_objs="$c_target_objs default-c.o"
   cxx_target_objs="$cxx_target_objs default-c.o"
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gcc/config/linux-peios.h
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
